    "text": "\n000\nFXUS65 KVEF 280256\nAFDVEF\n\nArea Forecast Discussion\nNational Weather Service Las Vegas NV\n700 PM PST Fri Nov 27 2026\n\n.KEY MESSAGES...\n\n* Above-normal temperatures and light winds are expected through \n  most of the weekend. \n\n* A Pacific storm system could bring gusty winds, widespread rain, \n  and mountain snow to the region early to midweek.\n\n&&\n\n.DISCUSSION...This afternoon through Thursday.\n\nHigh pressure centered off the coast of southern California will \nspread across the Desert Southwest this weekend, bringing mainly \nlight winds and warm temperatures. Readings will continue to climb \nthrough Sunday, when afternoon highs will reach 7 to 10 degrees \nabove normal. Winds will remain generally light through tomorrow \nnight. However, an approaching Pacific trough could bring a period \nof gusty winds along the eastern slopes of the Sierra and across the \nwestern Mojave Desert Sunday evening and night. The latest \nprobabilistic guidance continues to show a 20 to 40 percent chance \nof gusts reaching 40 mph along portions of US 395 in the Owens \nValley and a 40 to 60 percent chance of gusts reaching 40 mph across \nthe western Mojave Desert north of Barstow.   \n\n\nUnsettled weather will return to the region early next week as a \nclosed low pushes into southern California from the eastern Pacific. \nThe latest guidance continues to trend farther south, with the \nensemble means bringing the center inland just north of San Diego. \nTiming has not changed significantly, with the greatest impacts \nstill expected Tuesday and Wednesday. With the more southerly track, \nexpected wind impacts have decreased compared to previous forecasts, \nwith the best chance for gusty winds Tuesday and Tuesday night \nacross San Bernardino, far southern Clark, and Mohave counties. \nPrecipitation will begin to move into the southern Great Basin \nMonday night into Tuesday morning. It will then continue to spread \neastward across the remainder of the region through Tuesday. There \nremains a substantial spread in forecast precipitation totals for \nTuesday through Wednesday, with a 90 percent chance of at least 0.25 \ninches and a 15 percent chance of more than 2 inches in desert \nvalleys. Snow will be possible at higher elevations, generally above \n7000 feet, but confidence remains too low to provide specific \naccumulation amounts. Any additional southward shift in the system's \ntrack could change forecast precipitation amounts across the region. \nContinue to monitor the forecast through the weekend as the details \nbecome clearer. \n&&\n\n.AVIATION...For Harry Reid...For the 06Z Forecast Package...Winds \nwill remain light and follow typical diurnal directional patterns \nthrough  Saturday night. Wind speeds are expected to remain below 8 \nknots, with only a 10 to 15 percent chance of seeing speeds exceed 8 \nknots for brief periods Saturday afternoon. VFR conditions will \nprevail, with SCT to BKN clouds and bases generally remaining 15kft \nAGL or higher. \n\nFor the rest of southern Nevada, northwest Arizona and southeast \nCalifornia...For the 06Z Forecast Package...Light winds following \ntypical diurnal wind patterns will continue across the majority of \nthe region through Saturday evening. The exception will continue to \nbe in the Colorado River Valley near KIFP, where northerly winds of \n10 to 15 knots are expected again Saturday afternoon. VFR conditions \nwill prevail across all areas, with SCT to BKN clouds and bases \ngenerally remaining 15kft AGL or higher through the evening. \n\n&&\n\n\n.SPOTTER INFORMATION STATEMENT...Spotters are encouraged to report\nany significant weather or impacts according to standard operating\nprocedures.\n\n&&\n\n$$\n\nDISCUSSION...Planz\nAVIATION...Planz/CO\n\nFor more forecast information...see us on our webpage:\nhttps://weather.gov/lasvegas or follow us on Facebook and Twitter\n",
